Same thing I mentioned on the tomcat-jdbc and DBCP versions of this: `serverAddress` can be a literal IPv6 address, and appending `:port` straight onto it gives `2001:db8::1:5432`, which reads as a bare address rather than address + port. Worth bracketing here, the same way `UrlParsingUtils.buildShortUrl` does when it builds `db.connection_string`. #19366 has landed, so on main `getServerAddress()` comes back unbracketed; the extra `startsWith("[")` check keeps this correct until you merge that in.

```suggestion
    StringBuilder result = new StringBuilder();
    if (serverAddress != null) {
      if (serverAddress.indexOf(':') >= 0 && !serverAddress.startsWith("[")) {
        result.append('[').append(serverAddress).append(']');
      } else {
        result.append(serverAddress);
      }
      if (serverPort != null) {
        result.append(':').append(serverPort);
      }
    }
```

Could you also add a name-derivation test with an IPv6 URL, e.g. `jdbc:postgresql://[2001:db8::1]:5432/orders` -> `[2001:db8::1]:5432/orders`?

